(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test02_2B"] = [{"id":"6c9f1727","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"One popular theory of the origin of the Moon, the “big whack,” posits that a protoplanet called Theia collided with Earth, flinging debris into orbit that eventually coalesced into the Moon. Until recently, Theia was ______, but researcher Qian Yuan and colleagues now claim to have identified pieces of the protoplanet in the lowermost section of Earth’s mantle.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"One popular theory of the origin of the Moon, the “big whack,” posits that a protoplanet called Theia collided with Earth, flinging debris into orbit","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that eventually coalesced into the Moon. Until recently, Theia was ______, but researcher Qian Yuan and colleagues now claim to have identified","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pieces of the protoplanet in the lowermost section of Earth’s mantle.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["desultory","spurious","veritable","notional"]},{"id":"c19b2f77","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Little is known about how plate tectonics — wherein slabs of Earth’s crust move over, under, away from, and against one another — began. Some researchers contend that tectonic movements began around 3 billion years ago, often noting that computer models of Earth’s mantle temperature at the time indicate that the mantle would have been sufficiently molten to enable the plates to move. Text 2 Ultimately, any plausible claim about the inception of tectonic movement must rest on empirical evidence from the geological record. Researcher Wriju Chowdhury and his team analyzed the geochemistry of zircon crystals to gain insight into the chemical composition of the magma from which the crystals formed and,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Little is known about how plate tectonics—wherein slabs of Earth’s crust move over, under, away from, and against one another—began. Some","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"researchers contend that tectonic movements began around 3 billion years ago, often noting that computer models of Earth’s mantle","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"temperature at the time indicate that the mantle would have been sufficiently molten to enable the plates to move.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Ultimately, any plausible claim about the inception of tectonic movement must rest on empirical evidence from the geological record. Researcher","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Wriju Chowdhury and his team analyzed the geochemistry of zircon crystals to gain insight into the chemical composition of the magma from","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"which the crystals formed and, based on the data, compellingly argue that plate tectonics may have been occurring as early as 4.2 billion years","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"ago.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to what “some researchers contend” as described in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that the temperature of Earth’s mantle 3 billion years ago was likely insufficient to allow for the level of tectonic movement predicted by computer models","By distinguishing between computer models of Earth’s mantle temperature that reliably predict the onset of plate tectonics and those that do not","By indicating that computer models of Earth’s mantle temperature are still being improved such that new models tend to be much more reliable than their predecessors","By asserting that a more definitive form of evidence than the computer models suggests a different timeline for the onset of plate tectonics on Earth"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Some","researchers contend"]},{"id":"a086c2cb","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Built in the 1970s, Raccoon Mountain is a pumped-storage hydropower facility (a water-battery ) located in the United States along the Tennessee River. When energy demand is low, excess power from the regional electric utility’s nuclear plants is used to pump water (from a lower reservoir filled from the Tennessee River) up a shaft to the summit lake, where the water is stored as gravitational potential energy. When energy demand peaks, the water drains down from the summit lake, spinning turbines and generating upward of 1,700 megawatts of power — enough to power one million homes for twenty hours.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Built in the 1970s, Raccoon Mountain is a pumped-storage hydropower facility (a “water-battery”) located in the United States along the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Tennessee River. When energy demand is low, excess power from the regional electric utility’s nuclear plants is used to pump water (from a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"lower reservoir filled from the Tennessee River) up a shaft to the summit lake, where the water is stored as gravitational potential energy. When","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"energy demand peaks, the water drains down from the summit lake, spinning turbines and generating upward of 1,700 megawatts of power—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"enough to power one million homes for twenty hours.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To point out the differences between two methods of energy generation","To explain the basics of how a specific energy technology works","To encourage regional electric utilities to build energy storage facilities","To discuss the benefits of a new energy technology"]},{"id":"3566120b","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde’s 1895 play The Importance of Being Earnest. CECILY: Have we got to part? ALGERNON: I am afraid so. It’s a very painful parting. CECILY: It is always painful to part from people whom one has known for a very brief space of time. The absence of old friends one can endure with equanimity. But even a momentary separation from anyone to whom one has just been introduced is almost unbearable. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde’s 1895 play The Importance of Being Earnest.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"CECILY: Have we got to part?","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"ALGERNON: I am afraid so. It’s a very painful parting.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"CECILY: It is always painful to part from people whom one has known for a very brief space of time. The absence of old friends one can endure","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"with equanimity. But even a momentary separation from anyone to whom one has just been introduced is almost unbearable.","indent":0}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “endure” most nearly mean?","choices":["Regret","Persist","Tolerate","Encourage"],"underline":"endure","underline_segments":["endure"]},{"id":"f4166aae","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"In addition to being an accomplished psychologist himself, Francis Cecil Sumner was a ______ increasing the opportunity for Black students to study psychology, helping to found the psychology department at Howard University, a historically Black university, in 1930.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In addition to being an accomplished psychologist himself, Francis Cecil Sumner was a ______ increasing the opportunity for Black students to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"study psychology, helping to found the psychology department at Howard University, a historically Black university, in 1930.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["proponent of","supplement to","beneficiary of","distraction for"]},{"id":"03080769","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Philosopher G.E. Moore’s most influential work entails the concept of common sense. He asserts that there are certain beliefs that all people, including philosophers, know instinctively to be true, whether or not they profess otherwise: among them, that they have bodies, or that they exist in a world with other objects that have three dimensions. Moore’s careful work on common sense may seem obvious but was in fact groundbreaking. Text 2 External world skepticism is a philosophical stance supposing that we cannot be sure of the existence of anything outside our own minds. During a lecture, G.E. Moore once offered a proof refuting this stance by holding out his hands and saying, “Here is one hand, and here is another.” Many philosophers reflexively reject this proof (Annalisa Coliva called it an obviously annoying failure ) but have found it a challenge to articulate exactly why the proof fails.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Philosopher G.E. Moore’s most influential work entails the concept of common sense. He asserts that there are certain beliefs that all people,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"including philosophers, know instinctively to be true, whether or not they profess otherwise: among them, that they have bodies, or that they exist","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in a world with other objects that have three dimensions. Moore’s careful work on common sense may seem obvious but was in fact","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"groundbreaking.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"External world skepticism is a philosophical stance supposing that we cannot be sure of the existence of anything outside our own minds.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"During a lecture, G.E. Moore once offered a proof refuting this stance by holding out his hands and saying, “Here is one hand, and here is","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"another.” Many philosophers reflexively reject this proof (Annalisa Coliva called it “an obviously annoying failure”) but have found it a challenge to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"articulate exactly why the proof fails.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 1 most likely respond to proponents of the philosophical stance outlined in Text 2?","choices":["By agreeing with those proponents that Moore’s treatment of positions that contradict his own is fundamentally unserious","By suggesting that an instinctive distaste for Moore’s position is preventing external world skeptics from constructing a sufficiently rigorous refutation of Moore","By arguing that if it is valid to assert that some facts are true based on instinct, it is also valid to assert that some proofs are inadequate based on instinct","By pointing out that Moore would assert that external world skepticism is at odds with other beliefs those proponents must unavoidably hold"]},{"id":"eaea6f8f","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Ordinary soap bubbles usually exist for a minute or less before popping due to either a rupture forced by gravity-induced drainage or the evaporation of the liquid from which the bubble is composed. But physicist Aymeric Roux and colleagues discovered ways to mitigate these factors, resulting in bubbles that can last for a year or more. For example, glycerol tends to adhere to water molecules, so a bubble with a shell that contains both water and glycerol is able to draw additional water molecules from the surrounding air and thereby compensate for evaporation.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Ordinary soap bubbles usually exist for a minute or less before popping due to either a rupture forced by gravity-induced drainage or the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"evaporation of the liquid from which the bubble is composed. But physicist Aymeric Roux and colleagues discovered ways to mitigate these","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"factors, resulting in bubbles that can last for a year or more. For example, glycerol tends to adhere to water molecules, so a bubble with a shell","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that contains both water and glycerol is able to draw additional water molecules from the surrounding air and thereby compensate for","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"evaporation.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the purpose of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the effects of a process devised by researchers that increases the longevity of an object discussed in the text.","It details the circumstances that prompted the research discussed in the text.","It presents a reason why the phenomenon discussed in the text that the researchers wanted to avoid will inevitably occur.","It mentions a method discussed in the text that researchers intend to test in future experiments."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["a bubble with a shell","that contains both water and glycerol is able to draw additional water molecules from the surrounding air and thereby compensate for","evaporation."]},{"id":"a2f64e58","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Individual elephants and Arctic herbivores such as caribou tend to have fixed geographic ranges throughout their lifetimes, which had prompted some researchers to speculate that the Arctic woolly mammoth, an extinct elephantid, might have exhibited similar behavior. Mammoth tusks grew in sequential layers, incorporating ingested minerals and organics, and so each ivory stratum reflects the ratio of strontium isotopes 87 86 ( Sr/ Sr) in the local environment; thus, the sequence of strata shows where the animal roamed during life. Recent analysis of the strontium ratios in the strata of one Arctic woolly mammoth tusk in relation to the geographic distribution of strontium ratios in the environment shows the animal’s range begin to expand as it reached sexual maturity, only to contract again in its final 1.5 years.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Individual elephants and Arctic herbivores such as caribou tend to have fixed geographic ranges throughout their lifetimes, which had prompted","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"some researchers to speculate that the Arctic woolly mammoth, an extinct elephantid, might have exhibited similar behavior. Mammoth tusks","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"grew in sequential layers, incorporating ingested minerals and organics, and so each ivory stratum reflects the ratio of strontium isotopes","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"87 86","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"( Sr/ Sr) in the local environment; thus, the sequence of strata shows where the animal roamed during life. Recent analysis of the strontium","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"ratios in the strata of one Arctic woolly mammoth tusk in relation to the geographic distribution of strontium ratios in the environment shows the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"animal’s range begin to expand as it reached sexual maturity, only to contract again in its final 1.5 years.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined statement in the text as a whole?","choices":["It discusses a characteristic shared by certain animals in order to explain why researchers raised a possibility that turned out not to be supported by data described later in the text.","It illustrates a pattern of behavior among certain animals in order to present a theory about exceptions to that pattern that is weakened by a finding described later in the text.","It describes a similarity in the behavior of certain animals in order to show why a method described later in the text did not reveal whether another animal also showed that behavior.","It introduces a trait shared by certain animals in order to contextualize a hypothesis about the origin of that trait that is advanced later in the text."],"underline":"Individual elephants and Arctic herbivores such as caribou tend to have fixed geographic ranges throughout their lifetimes,","underline_segments":["Individual elephants and Arctic herbivores such as caribou tend to have fixed geographic ranges throughout their lifetimes,"]},{"id":"4b3d6062","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"The Mammillaria cactus M. boolii occurs naturally only in the state of Sonora in Mexico, and the smallness of its range makes it especially vulnerable to extinction. The traditional single-species approach to conservation emphasizes the need to focus on individual species most at risk, like M. boolii, but recently, conservationists have argued that an ecosystem-based approach that incorporates the many interactions between the climate, terrain, and various species of a given geographical area may lead to better outcomes for all the species in a given location. If this view is correct, the single-species approach to the conservation of M. boolii could thus ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Mammillaria cactus M. boolii occurs naturally only in the state of Sonora in Mexico, and the smallness of its range makes it especially","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"vulnerable to extinction. The traditional single-species approach to conservation emphasizes the need to focus on individual species most at","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"risk, like M. boolii, but recently, conservationists have argued that an ecosystem-based approach that incorporates the many interactions","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"between the climate, terrain, and various species of a given geographical area may lead to better outcomes for all the species in a given location.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"If this view is correct, the single-species approach to the conservation of M. boolii could thus ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["lead to a better understanding of how the distribution of Mammillaria species throughout Mexico has affected their survival.","allow conservationists to better consider how climatic changes affecting Sonora may reduce the number of species competing with M. boolii.","erroneously shift the focus of conservation efforts away from M. boolii itself.","fail to consider the ways in which the survival of M. boolii may be influenced by changes in the populations of other species that inhabit Sonora."]},{"id":"068f939b","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"The ancient Greek concept of “mimesis,” a term used in the works of Plato, Aristotle, and other Greek philosophers in discussions of representational art — visual, performance, or literary art that aims to depict the real world — is a foundational concept of the Western philosophy of aesthetics. Mimesis is typically translated as “imitation” in modern editions of ancient Greek texts, but scholar Stephen Halliwell warns that this is overly reductive: “imitation” implies that art merely copies —and is thus by definition entirely derivative of — a reality that exists outside and prior to the work of art, and translating “mimesis” thusly obscures the multifaceted ways in which the ancient Greeks understood the relationship between art and reality.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The ancient Greek concept of “mimesis,” a term used in the works of Plato, Aristotle, and other Greek philosophers in discussions of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"representational art—visual, performance, or literary art that aims to depict the real world—is a foundational concept of the Western philosophy","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of aesthetics. Mimesis is typically translated as “imitation” in modern editions of ancient Greek texts, but scholar Stephen Halliwell warns that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"this is overly reductive: “imitation” implies that art merely copies—and is thus by definition entirely derivative of—a reality that exists outside and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"prior to the work of art, and translating “mimesis” thusly obscures the multifaceted ways in which the ancient Greeks understood the relationship","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"between art and reality.","indent":0}],"question":"Which statement, if true, would most directly support the claim by Halliwell presented in the text?","choices":["One of the earliest appearances of mimesis’s root word, mimos, can be found in an ancient Greek tragedy in reference to dramatic impersonation, and the mim- root came to be generally associated with the musical and poetic arts by the fifth century BCE.","Both Plato’s and Aristotle’s theorizations of mimesis examine the psychological effects that works of art induce in the viewer or listener.","Although several of Plato’s earliest philosophical works discuss aesthetic ideas, the term “mimesis” doesn’t appear in Plato’s discussions of art until Cratylus, a relatively late work.","Although Plato’s writings typically characterize representational art as an inferior reflection of the physical world, Aristotle suggests that mimesis can refer to art’s capacity to envision hypothetical conditions that could, but don’t yet, exist."]},{"id":"dc3ea63e","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"To dye wool, Navajo (Diné) weaver Lillie Taylor uses plants and vegetables from Arizona, where she lives. For example, she achieved the deep reds and browns featured in her 2003 rug In the Path of the Four Seasons by using Arizona dock roots, drying and grinding them before mixing the powder with water to create a dye bath. To intensify the appearance of certain colors, Taylor also sometimes mixes in clay obtained from nearby soil.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"To dye wool, Navajo (Diné) weaver Lillie Taylor uses plants and vegetables from Arizona, where she lives. For example, she achieved the deep","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"reds and browns featured in her 2003 rug In the Path of the Four Seasons by using Arizona dock roots, drying and grinding them before mixing the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"powder with water to create a dye bath. To intensify the appearance of certain colors, Taylor also sometimes mixes in clay obtained from nearby","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"soil.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Reds and browns are not commonly featured in most of Taylor’s rugs.","Taylor draws on local resources in the approach she uses to dye wool.","Taylor finds it difficult to locate Arizona dock root in the desert.","In the Path of the Four Seasons is widely acclaimed for its many colors and innovative weaving techniques."]},{"id":"11c00ab9","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Estimated Impacts of Invasive Predator Species on Threatened Birds, Mammals, and Reptiles Invasive predator Estimated impact on threatened Estimated impact on threatened Estimated impact on threatened species birds mammals reptiles Mongooses 0.66 0.75 0.44 Dogs 0.67 0.57 0.44 Cats 0.74 0.61 0.52 How harmful are invasive predators to threatened species? To find out, researchers assigned values from 0.25 to 1.00 to various harms caused by invasive predators. The researchers then used these values to estimate the extent to which several invasive predator species have harmfully impacted threatened birds, mammals, and reptiles. In the table, higher numbers represent greater impacts. The estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals is ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Estimated Impacts of Invasive Predator Species on Threatened Birds, Mammals, and Reptiles","headers":["Invasive predator species","Estimated impact on threatened birds","Estimated impact on threatened mammals","Estimated impact on threatened reptiles"],"rows":[["Mongooses","0.66","0.75","0.44"],["Dogs","0.67","0.57","0.44"],["Cats","0.74","0.61","0.52"]]},{"type":"text","text":"How harmful are invasive predators to threatened species? To find out, researchers assigned values from 0.25 to 1.00 to various harms caused by invasive predators. The researchers then used these values to estimate the extent to which several invasive predator species have harmfully impacted threatened birds, mammals, and reptiles. In the table, higher numbers represent greater impacts. The estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals is ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?","choices":["0.35.","0.61.","0.75.","0.52."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Estimated Impacts of Invasive Predator","Species on Threatened Birds, Mammals,","and Reptiles"]},{"id":"5d122d45","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Psychologists Dacher Keltner and Jonathan Haidt have argued that experiencing awe — a sensation of reverence and wonder typically brought on by perceiving something grand or powerful — can enable us to feel more connected to others and thereby inspire us to act more altruistically. Keltner, along with Paul K. Piff, Pia Dietze, and colleagues, claims to have found evidence for this effect in a recent study where participants were asked to either gaze up at exceptionally tall trees in a nearby grove (reported to be a universally awe-inspiring experience) or stare at the exterior of a nearby, nondescript building. After one minute, an experimenter deliberately spilled a box of pens nearby. Which finding from","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Psychologists Dacher Keltner and Jonathan Haidt have argued that experiencing awe—a sensation of reverence and wonder typically brought on","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"by perceiving something grand or powerful—can enable us to feel more connected to others and thereby inspire us to act more altruistically.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Keltner, along with Paul K. Piff, Pia Dietze, and colleagues, claims to have found evidence for this effect in a recent study where participants were","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"asked to either gaze up at exceptionally tall trees in a nearby grove (reported to be a universally awe-inspiring experience) or stare at the exterior","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of a nearby, nondescript building. After one minute, an experimenter deliberately spilled a box of pens nearby.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding from the researchers’ study, if true, would most strongly support their claim?","choices":["Participants who had been looking at the trees helped the experimenter pick up significantly more pens than did participants who had been looking at the building.","Participants who helped the experimenter pick up the pens used a greater number of positive words to describe the trees and the building in a postexperiment survey than did participants who did not help the experimenter.","Participants who did not help the experimenter pick up the pens were significantly more likely to report having experienced a feeling of awe, regardless of whether they looked at the building or the trees.","Participants who had been looking at the building were significantly more likely to notice that the experimenter had dropped the pens than were participants who had been looking at the trees."]},{"id":"af125459","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Number and Origin of Clamshell Tools Found at Different Depths below the Surface in Neanderthal Cave Depth of tools found below surface in cave Clamshells that Neanderthals collected from Clamshells that Neanderthals harvested from (meters) the beach the seafloor 2– 3 7 0 3– 4 99 33 4– 5 2 0 5– 6 18 7 6– 7 1 0 Two kinds of clamshell tools used by Neanderthals were dug up in a cave on the western coast of Italy. Archaeologist Paola Villa and her colleagues studied the tools and determined that Neanderthals either collected clams that had washed onto the beach or harvested clams from the seafloor and then sharpened the shells to make tools. The highest number of tools made from clamshells that were collected from the beach was found at a depth of ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Number and Origin of Clamshell Tools Found at Different Depths below the Surface in Neanderthal Cave","headers":["Depth of tools found below surface in cave (meters)","Clamshells that Neanderthals collected from the beach","Clamshells that Neanderthals harvested from the seafloor"],"rows":[["2–3","7","0"],["3–4","99","33"],["4–5","2","0"],["5–6","18","7"],["6–7","1","0"]]},{"type":"text","text":"Two kinds of clamshell tools used by Neanderthals were dug up in a cave on the western coast of Italy. Archaeologist Paola Villa and her colleagues studied the tools and determined that Neanderthals either collected clams that had washed onto the beach or harvested clams from the seafloor and then sharpened the shells to make tools. The highest number of tools made from clamshells that were collected from the beach was found at a depth of ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?","choices":["5 6 meters below the surface. –","4 5 meters below the surface. –","3 4 meters below the surface. –","6 7 meters below the surface."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Number and Origin of","Clamshell Tools Found at Different Depths below the","the Surface in Neanderthal Cave"]},{"id":"a9ac31e4","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Area of Three Glaciers in the 2016 Swiss Glacier Inventory 50 40 Area (square km) 30 20 10 0 Glacier Gorner Fiescher Unteraar To monitor changes to glaciers in Switzerland, the government periodically measures them for features like total area of ice and mean ice thickness, which are then reported in the Swiss Glacier Inventory. These measurements can be used to compare the glaciers. For example, the Gorner glacier had ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Area of Three Glaciers in the 2016 Swiss Glacier Inventory — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"To monitor changes to glaciers in Switzerland, the government periodically measures them for features like total area of ice and mean ice thickness, which are then reported in the Swiss Glacier Inventory. These measurements can be used to compare the glaciers. For example, the Gorner glacier had ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the example?","choices":["a larger area than either the Fiescher glacier or the Unteraar glacier.","a smaller area than the Fiescher glacier but a larger area than the Unteraar glacier.","a smaller area than either the Fiescher glacier or the Unteraar glacier.","a larger area than the Fiescher glacier but a smaller area than the Unteraar glacier."],"unusable":"missing graph"},{"id":"4dcedc31","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"For scientists to produce new iron nanoparticles of a specific size or strength, they must first be able to monitor reactions between oxygen and existing iron nanoparticles at a near-atomic level of detail. Fortunately, chemistry ______ and his colleagues at Temple University recently developed a new approach that does just that.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"For scientists to produce new iron nanoparticles of a specific size or strength, they must first be able to monitor reactions between oxygen and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"existing iron nanoparticles at a near-atomic level of detail. Fortunately, chemistry ______ and his colleagues at Temple University recently","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"developed a new approach that does just that.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["professor Yugang Sun","professor Yugang Sun,","professor, Yugang Sun,","professor, Yugang Sun"]},{"id":"b1e8b87f","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Winding through the ice atop Norway’s Jotunheim Mountains is the Lendbreen pass, an ancient route that was used by hunters, farmers, traders, and travelers in the Middle Ages before eventually falling into disuse. Recently, archeologists have ______ knives, horseshoes, wool tunics, and thousands of other artifacts from the icy pass.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Winding through the ice atop Norway’s Jotunheim Mountains is the Lendbreen pass, an ancient route that was used by hunters, farmers, traders,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and travelers in the Middle Ages before eventually falling into disuse. Recently, archeologists have ______ knives, horseshoes, wool tunics, and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"thousands of other artifacts from the icy pass.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["unearthed:","unearthed,","unearthed —","unearthed"]},{"id":"de3dd17d","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Planetary scientist Briony Horgan and her colleagues have determined that as much as 25 percent of the sand on Mars is composed of impact spherules. These spherical bits of glass form when asteroids collide with the planet, ejecting bits of molten rock into the atmosphere that, after cooling and solidifying into glass, ______ back onto Mars’s surface.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Planetary scientist Briony Horgan and her colleagues have determined that as much as 25 percent of the sand on Mars is composed of impact","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"spherules. These spherical bits of glass form when asteroids collide with the planet, ejecting bits of molten rock into the atmosphere that, after","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cooling and solidifying into glass, ______ back onto Mars’s surface.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["to rain","raining","having rained","rain"]},{"id":"b32eab9f","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Increased gender diversity is revitalizing the field of economics, according to Harvard’s Claudia Goldin. The trailblazing accomplishments of Goldin, winner of the 2023 Nobel Prize in Economics for her work on women in the labor force, ______ to the value of scholars of diverse backgrounds in spurring research into previously unexplored, but vitally important, topics.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Increased gender diversity is revitalizing the field of economics, according to Harvard’s Claudia Goldin. The trailblazing accomplishments of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Goldin, winner of the 2023 Nobel Prize in Economics for her work on women in the labor force, ______ to the value of scholars of diverse","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"backgrounds in spurring research into previously unexplored, but vitally important, topics.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["attests","has attested","is attesting","attest"]},{"id":"9902d2de","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"The Alvarez theory, developed in 1980 by physicist Luis Walter Alvarez and his geologist son Walter Alvarez, maintained that the secondary effects of an asteroid impact caused many dinosaurs and other animals to die ______ it left unexplored the question of whether unrelated volcanic activity might have also contributed to the mass extinctions.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Alvarez theory, developed in 1980 by physicist Luis Walter Alvarez and his geologist son Walter Alvarez, maintained that the secondary","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"effects of an asteroid impact caused many dinosaurs and other animals to die ______ it left unexplored the question of whether unrelated","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"volcanic activity might have also contributed to the mass extinctions.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["out but","out, but","out","out,"]},{"id":"a2293b3f","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"A harpsichord may look just like a piano, but the difference between the two instruments is easy to hear. When a harpsichord’s keys are pressed, the strings inside the ______ are plucked, not struck.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A harpsichord may look just like a piano, but the difference between the two instruments is easy to hear. When a harpsichord’s keys are pressed,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the strings inside the ______ are plucked, not struck.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["instrument:","instrument","instrument —","instrument,"]},{"id":"70538b9a","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In the 1930s, the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute in India sought to limit the country’s dependence on imported sugarcane. The institute enlisted botanist Janaki Ammal to breed a local variety of sugarcane. She crossbred the imported sugarcane species Saccharum officinarum with grasses native to India. She succeeded in creating sugarcane hybrids well suited to India’s climate. The student wants to emphasize Janaki Ammal’s achievement.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In the 1930s, the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute in India sought to limit the country’s dependence on imported sugarcane.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The institute enlisted botanist Janaki Ammal to breed a local variety of sugarcane.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"She crossbred the imported sugarcane species Saccharum officinarum with grasses native to India.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"She succeeded in creating sugarcane hybrids well suited to India’s climate.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["By crossbreeding the imported sugarcane species Saccharum officinarum with grasses native to India, Ammal succeeded in creating sugarcane hybrids well suited to India’s climate.","In the 1930s, the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute, which enlisted Ammal, sought to limit dependence on imported sugarcane.","Ammal was enlisted by the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute at a time when a local variety of sugarcane needed to be produced.","As part of efforts to breed a local variety of sugarcane, an imported sugarcane species called Saccharum officinarum was crossbred with grasses native to India."]},{"id":"5222ffab","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Neuroscientists Krishnan Padmanabhan and Zhen Chen sought to better understand the workings of the brain’s olfactory system. They devised a study using mathematical models. They found that certain fibers allow the brain to toggle from one method of processing smells to another. In one method, cells in the piriform cortex (where the perception of odor forms) capture olfactory information at a given moment. In the other, the cells track changes in olfactory information over time. The student wants to summarize the study’s findings.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Neuroscientists Krishnan Padmanabhan and Zhen Chen sought to better understand the workings of the brain’s olfactory system.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"They devised a study using mathematical models.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"They found that certain fibers allow the brain to toggle from one method of processing smells to another.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In one method, cells in the piriform cortex (where the perception of odor forms) capture olfactory information at a given moment.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In the other, the cells track changes in olfactory information over time.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To arrive at these findings, which describe dual methods of processing smells in the piriform cortex, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study using mathematical models.","Padmanabhan and Chen showed that olfactory information is captured by cells in the piriform cortex, where the perception of odor forms.","Using mathematical models, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study to better understand the workings of the brain’s olfactory system.","According to Padmanabhan and Chen, the brain can toggle between capturing olfactory information at a given moment and tracking changes in that information over time."]},{"id":"56b000d0","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The factors that affect clutch size (the number of eggs laid at one time) have been well studied in birds but not in lizards. A team led by Shai Meiri of Tel Aviv University investigated which factors influence lizard clutch size. Meiri’s team obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models. Larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change. Lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions. The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The factors that affect clutch size (the number of eggs laid at one time) have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A team led by Shai Meiri of Tel Aviv University investigated which factors influence lizard clutch size.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Meiri’s team obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Researchers wanted to know which factors influence lizard egg clutch size because such factors have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.","After they obtained data for over 3,900 lizard species, researchers determined that larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.","We now know that lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.","Researchers obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models."]},{"id":"17ec916d","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Bharati Mukherjee was an Indian-born author of novels and short stories. She published the novel The Holder of the World in 1993. A central character in the novel is a woman living in twentieth-century United States. Another central character is a woman living in seventeenth-century India. The student wants to introduce the novel The Holder of the World to an audience already familiar with Bharati Mukherjee.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Bharati Mukherjee was an Indian-born author of novels and short stories.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"She published the novel The Holder of the World in 1993.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"A central character in the novel is a woman living in twentieth-century United States.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Another central character is a woman living in seventeenth-century India.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Bharati Mukherjee’s settings include both twentieth-century United States and seventeenth-century India.","In addition to her novel The Holder of the World, which was published in 1993, Indian-born author Bharati Mukherjee wrote other novels and short stories.","Bharati Mukherjee’s novel The Holder of the World centers around two women, one living in twentieth-century United States and the other in seventeenth-century India.","The Holder of the World was not the only novel written by Indian-born author Bharati Mukherjee."]},{"id":"e8494245","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Political scientist Graham Allison is known for his Thucydides trap theory. Allison’s theory states that whenever “a rising power is threatening to displace a ruling power,” conflict is likely. The theory is based on Thucydides’s explanation of the conflict between Athens and Sparta. Thucydides wrote that “the rise of Athens and the fear this instilled in Sparta” made conflict “inevitable.” History professor Edmund Stewart recently challenged the historical basis of the theory. Stewart claimed that Athens was not a rising power and that the rivals experienced a “clash of cultures” instead. The student wants to use a quotation to challenge Thucydides’s explanation of the conflict between Athens and Sparta.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Political scientist Graham Allison is known for his Thucydides trap theory.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Allison’s theory states that whenever “a rising power is threatening to displace a ruling power,” conflict is likely.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The theory is based on Thucydides’s explanation of the conflict between Athens and Sparta.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Thucydides wrote that “the rise of Athens and the fear this instilled in Sparta” made conflict “inevitable.”","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"History professor Edmund Stewart recently challenged the historical basis of the theory.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Stewart claimed that Athens was not a rising power and that the rivals experienced a “clash of cultures” instead.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["According to Allison’s Thucydides trap theory, whenever “a rising power is threatening to displace a ruling power,” conflict is likely.","Thucydides wrote that conflict between the two powers was “inevitable,” although Stewart later challenged the historical basis of this claim.","According to Stewart, a “clash of cultures” between Athens and Sparta caused the conflict, not Athens’s rise.","Thucydides explained that conflict was caused by “the rise of Athens and the fear this instilled in Sparta,” but Allison disagreed, seeing the conflict as an example of the Thucydides trap."]},{"id":"b5cd28a7","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Samuel Delany is a US writer known for his science fiction. Delany’s science fiction novel Babel-17 was published in 1966. The novel won a Nebula Award in 1967. The Nebula Awards are given each year to the best works of science fiction published in the US. The student wants to indicate the title of a novel that won a Nebula Award.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Samuel Delany is a US writer known for his science fiction.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Delany’s science fiction novel Babel-17 was published in 1966.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The novel won a Nebula Award in 1967.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The Nebula Awards are given each year to the best works of science fiction published in the US.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Babel-17, by Samuel Delany, won a Nebula Award in 1967.","Samuel Delany published a science fiction novel in 1966.","Samuel Delany is an award-winning US writer known for his science fiction.","One of Samuel Delany’s novels was among the best works of science fiction published in the US."]}];
